{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017RSmspUYXqVxT2XL45e88N","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\n     2→# Aquarium / Planted Tank Ecosystem Simulator — Implementation Spec (Rust)\n     3→\n     4→Version: 0.1  \n     5→Target system: freshwater planted shrimp tank (`Neocaridina davidi`)  \n     6→Primary UI: terminal / text UI  \n     7→Core requirement: scientifically grounded, deterministic, agent-implementable\n     8→\n     9→---\n    10→\n    11→## 1. Objective\n    12→\n    13→Build a Rust simulation engine that models a living freshwater planted aquarium as an ecosystem rather than a scripted set of events.\n    14→\n    15→The simulator must let a player:\n    16→\n    17→- configure tanks of different sizes and shapes\n    18→- stock and maintain a tank over time\n    19→- change husbandry variables, including **ambient temperature**\n    20→- observe chemistry, plant growth, algae, biofilm, microfauna, shrimp condition, and long-term stabilization\n    21→- encounter realistic emergent problems such as:\n    22→  - cycling delays\n    23→  - ammonia or nitrite stress\n    24→  - algae blooms\n    25→  - oxygen dips\n    26→  - breeding success/failure\n    27→  - molt issues\n    28→  - detritus accumulation\n    29→  - instability in small tanks compared with larger tanks\n    30→\n    31→The design must be specific enough that an implementation agent can build the system incrementally without re-architecting the codebase.\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→## 2. Scope\n    36→\n    37→## 2.1 In scope for v1\n    38→\n    39→A single freshwater tank containing:\n    40→\n    41→- water column\n    42→- tank geometry and water volume\n    43→- substrate\n    44→- filter / biomedia compartment\n    45→- heater (optional but supported)\n    46→- light\n    47→- rooted plants\n    48→- periphyton / biofilm\n    49→- free algae\n    50→- microbial guilds\n    51→- microfauna guilds\n    52→- shrimp population\n    53→- detritus / dissolved organics\n    54→- player maintenance actions\n    55→\n    56→## 2.2 Out of scope for v1\n    57→\n    58→- photorealistic graphics\n    59→- saltwater / reef chemistry\n    60→- disease-specific pathology models\n    61→- species-level microbial taxonomic simulation\n    62→- exact CFD, hydrodynamics, or molecular chemistry\n    63→- multiplayer\n    64→- online features\n    65→\n    66→## 2.3 Planned later\n    67→\n    68→- fish populations\n    69→- snails and other macro-grazers\n    70→- additional plant archetypes\n    71→- scenario editor\n    72→- richer rendering frontend\n    73→- more detailed carbonate chemistry and ion pairing\n    74→- external research calibration tools\n    75→\n    76→---\n    77→\n    78→## 3. Scientific modeling stance\n    79→\n    80→The simulation is **mechanistic and guild-based**.\n    81→\n    82→It must not attempt molecule-level or species-complete ecological simulation. Instead, it must model the main causal pathways that drive real aquarium outcomes:\n    83→\n    84→- waste generation -> ammonia -> nitrite -> nitrate\n    85→- pH and temperature effects on unionized ammonia risk\n    86→- oxygen and carbon cycling\n    87→- plant vs algae competition\n    88→- substrate and biofilm effects\n    89→- microfauna grazing and predation loops\n    90→- shrimp feeding, stress, molting, and reproduction\n    91→- tank-size-dependent stability\n    92→\n    93→Important scientific anchors for the model:\n    94→\n    95→- Unionized ammonia fraction increases with pH, and temperature affects ammonia toxicity.\n    96→- Home aquarium biofilters show real microbial succession and variable cycle times rather than one fixed “recipe.”\n    97→- Aquarium biofilters commonly include AOA and comammox `Nitrospira`, so nitrification should not be represented as instant or purely magical conversion.\n    98→- Submerged macrophytes can take nutrients from sediment and/or the water column.\n    99→- Rising temperature interacts with nutrient availability and can shift the plant/algae balance.\n   100→- Protozoan grazing affects bacterial communities and nutrient mineralization."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013yHGd5uFJHJgdiXSiSjdyf","session_id":"5afc8472-1402-44b3-9d7c-1a241eb51364","uuid":"6f782043-bee2-4be8-a6ba-97a6f9604e71"}
